#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:0
msgid ""
"Complete reference for Data locations in Snap and snapd, covering "
"configuration, behavior, requirements, and practical implementation details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:8
msgid "Data locations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:10
msgid ""
"A vast majority of snaps use strict confinement to isolate both their "
"execution environments and their data from your system (see {ref}`Snap "
"Confinement <explanation-security-snap-confinement>` for further details)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:12
msgid ""
"This confinement is an important security feature, but it can affect how and "
"where you access a snap's files, including:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:14
msgid "configuration files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:15
msgid "user documents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:16
msgid "internal databases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:18
msgid ""
"Access to files like these is fundamental to the operation of most "
"applications and snap confinement has been designed to always permit access "
"to snap-specific locations for these files when required. These locations "
"are described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:20
msgid "System data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:22
msgid ""
"Within the snap environment, environment variables are used to reference "
"different accessible locations.  The following variables and default "
"referenced locations are used to store system data:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:24
msgid ""
"**SNAP_COMMON**: `/var/snap/<snap name>/common`</br> This directory is owned "
"and writable by root and is used to store data that is common across "
"multiple {ref}`revisions <explanation-how-snaps-work-revisions>` of the snap "
"(e.g.: revision 6, revision 7, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:24
#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:27
#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:36
#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:39
msgid "</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:27
msgid ""
"**SNAP_ DATA**: `/var/snap/<snap name>/<revision number>`</br> This location "
"it is also used to store data, mostly information utilised by background "
"application and services, for logging, and other tasks that require "
"persistence between snap launches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:30
msgid ""
"A {ref}`snapshot <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-create-data-snapshots>` of "
"`SNAP_DATA` and `SNAP_COMMON` is created and restored when performing a snap "
"update (refresh) or revert operation. The contents of `SNAP_DATA` is "
"specific to the snap revision, while the contents of `SNAP_COMMON` is "
"applicable to all revisions and will overwrite the contents of `SNAP_COMMON` "
"when restored. See {ref}`What a snapshot stores <ref-create-data-"
"snapshots_what-a-snapshot-stores>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:32
msgid "User data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:34
msgid ""
"Snaps can also contain user data. As with the system data environment "
"variables, `SNAP_COMMON` and `SNAP_DATA`,  the following user-specific "
"environment variables point to directories for user data:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:36
msgid ""
"**SNAP_USER_COMMON**: `/home/<username>/snap/<snap name>/common`</br> This "
"location maps user data across each revision of a snap. This data is only "
"copied or backed-up through {ref}`Snapshots <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-"
"create-data-snapshots>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:39
msgid ""
"**SNAP_USER_DATA**: `/home/<username>/snap/<snap name>/<revision>`</br> This "
"contains any user data that the snap writes to its own home. This is *in "
"contrast* to what the Linux user would consider *their* home. It is "
"important to note this distinction, because it can be useful, and even "
"important when users decide to perform maintenance operations with their "
"snaps (like removal). By default, every snap will use a symlink *current*, "
"pointing to the latest available revision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:42
msgid ""
"Both `SNAP_USER_COMMON` and `SNAP_USER_DATA` only become available after a "
"snap has been run once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:45
msgid "Ubuntu Core"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:47
msgid ""
"On Ubuntu Core, the **`SNAP_SAVE_DATA`** environment variable within a "
"snap's environment points to a snap-specific location on the [ubuntu-save]"
"(https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/explanation/core-elements/inside-"
"ubuntu-core/#volume-layouts) volume. This is used to store data that can be "
"accessed during recovery or after re-installation of Ubuntu Core."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:49
msgid ""
"For example, in the reference Ubuntu Core 22 image, from within the _hello-"
"world_ snap, `SNAP_SAVE_DATA` has the following value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:51
msgid "SNAP_SAVE_DATA=/var/lib/snapd/save/snap/hello-world\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:55
msgid ""
"The above environment variable references a mount point at the following "
"location:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:57
msgid ""
"$ mount | grep \"snapd/save\"\n"
"/dev/mapper/ubuntu-save on /var/lib/snapd/save type ext4 (rw,relatime)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:62
msgid "Other locations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:64
msgid "There are several other directories you should be aware of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:66
msgid ""
"**`/var/lib/snapd/cache`** This is the working cache and is used to minimise "
"download size and speed-up refreshes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:68
msgid ""
"**`/var/lib/snapd/snaps`** Contains all the versions of snaps installed on "
"your system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:70
msgid ""
"**`/var/lib/snapd/snapshots/`** Contains both the manually generated and "
"automatically generated {ref}`snapshots <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-create-"
"data-snapshots>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:74
msgid "Snap installation locations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:76
msgid ""
"When a user {ref}`installs a snap <tutorials-get-started>` from the [Snap "
"Store](https://snapcraft.io/store), the following happens:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:78
msgid ""
"The {ref}`snapd services <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-control-services>` "
"downloads the snap as a single file – a SquashFS archive with a _.snap_ "
"suffix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:79
msgid ""
"The snap file is stored in `/var/lib/snaps/snaps`. It is then *loop mounted* "
"to `/snap` from where its contents become readable by the system. See {ref}"
"`The snap format <reference-development-yaml-schemas-the-snap-format>` for "
"further details on what is included in a snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:81
msgid ""
"By design, the read-only filesystem cannot provide a persistent experience "
"between application launches, which is why snaps also have writable parts "
"for system data and for user data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:83
msgid "Snap deletion data remnants"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:85
msgid ""
"When deleting and removing a snap from a system, the following will happen:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:87
msgid "The snap will be unmounted and no longer shown under `/snap`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:88
msgid ""
"The data under `/var/snap/<snap name>/` and `/home/<username>/snap/` will be "
"deleted. However, a copy is be retained as a {ref}`snapshot <how-to-guides-"
"manage-snaps-create-data-snapshots>` for 30-days (except on Ubuntu Core "
"systems), allowing data to be restored or manually retrieved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:90
msgid ""
"{ref}`Snapshot management <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-create-data-"
"snapshots>` can be used to restore data, or unzip the archives, and only "
"copy the data you consider necessary. With the right permissions, you can "
"also create your own backup routine, which copies the important data like "
"application databases, configurations or similar content to a backup path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:92
msgid ""
"To remove a snap without generating a snapshot, use the additional ‘–purge’ "
"argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/administration/data-locations.md:94
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap remove vlc --purge\n"
"vlc removed\n"
msgstr ""
